  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how the brain communicates with peripheral organs through the autonomic nervous system. A sagittal view of the brain highlights sensory and autonomic control regions, including the SFO, OVLT, hypothalamus/PVN, RVLM, and the NTS/DMV complex in the brainstem. These central sites appear to integrate signals and send outputs along two main pathways.\n\nOn the left, the sympathetic nervous system is shown projecting through the sympathetic chain to a wide range of targets. These include the carotid body, thymus, lymph nodes, heart, vasculature, adrenal gland, kidney, spleen, and bone marrow, emphasizing broad control over cardiovascular, endocrine, and immune-related tissues. On the right, the parasympathetic nervous system sends a more limited set of projections to the carotid body, heart, liver, stomach, and intestines, consistent with regulation of visceral organ function.\n\nThe dashed horizontal line separates “Brain” from “Periphery,” reinforcing the overall message that central autonomic circuits coordinate body-wide responses through distinct sympathetic and parasympathetic outputs. Overall, the image presents a high-level map of neuroanatomical connections linking brain sensing and integration centers to peripheral organs.",
